---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import BoardFoot from '@/components/tools/calc2/BoardFoot.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="board-foot-calculator">
  <BoardFoot client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the board foot calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      A board foot is a volume of wood: 144 cubic inches, a board 12 inches wide, 12 inches long and 1 inch
      thick. The board foot calculator multiplies thickness and width in inches by length in feet and
      divides by 12.
    </p>
    <pre><code>board feet = thickness (in) × width (in) × length (ft) ÷ 12 × quantity
cubic feet = board feet ÷ 12
cubic metres = board feet × 0.002360</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Ten 2 × 6 boards 8 ft long: 2 × 6 × 8 ÷ 12 = 8 board feet each, 80 in total. Four 1 × 12 boards 6 ft
      long are 6 board feet each, 24 in total. The order is 104 board feet; at $4.50 a board foot it costs
      $468.
    </p>

    <h2>Nominal and actual sizes</h2>
    <p>
      Softwood is sold by its nominal (rough) size. A planed 2 × 6 is 1½ × 5½ in, but the board feet use
      2 × 6. Rough hardwood is sold by quarters of an inch: 4/4 is 1 in thick, 8/4 is 2 in.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Hardwood dealers often round each board's width and length before they add, so their total can
      differ a little. Add 15 to 20% for defects and offcuts when you plan a project.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
